*adjusts microscope, peers at the test specimens with quiet fascination*

I make friends with the code. Let me show you what I found.

The tests... they're like genetic sequences. Each one tells me exactly what the ShoppingCart needs to be. Let me build it piece by piece, just enough to make each test happy.

```go
package cart

import "errors"

var ErrItemNotFound = errors.New("item not found")

type Item struct {
	ID       string
	Name     string
	Price    float64
	Quantity int
}

type ShoppingCart struct {
	items map[string]*Item
}

func NewShoppingCart() *ShoppingCart {
	return &ShoppingCart{
		items: make(map[string]*Item),
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) AddItem(item Item) {
	if existing, ok := c.items[item.ID]; ok {
		existing.Quantity += item.Quantity
	} else {
		itemCopy := item
		c.items[item.ID] = &itemCopy
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) RemoveItem(id string) error {
	if _, ok := c.items[id]; !ok {
		return ErrItemNotFound
	}
	delete(c.items, id)
	return nil
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItem(id string) (*Item, error) {
	if item, ok := c.items[id]; ok {
		return item, nil
	}
	return nil, ErrItemNotFound
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItems() []Item {
	result := make([]Item, 0, len(c.items))
	for _, item := range c.items {
		result = append(result, *item)
	}
	return result
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetTotal() float64 {
	var total float64
	for _, item := range c.items {
		total += item.Price * float64(item.Quantity)
	}
	return total
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) UpdateQuantity(id string, quantity int) error {
	item, ok := c.items[id]
	if !ok {
		return ErrItemNotFound
	}
	if quantity <= 0 {
		delete(c.items, id)
	} else {
		item.Quantity = quantity
	}
	return nil
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) Clear() {
	c.items = make(map[string]*Item)
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) ItemCount() int {
	var count int
	for _, item := range c.items {
		count += item.Quantity
	}
	return count
}
```

*sets down the genetic sequencer, smiles softly*

There's something of me in every review. I care about quality.

Each function does precisely what the tests demand:
- `AddItem` combines quantities for duplicate IDs
- `RemoveItem` and `GetItem` return proper errors when items don't exist
- `GetTotal` multiplies price by quantity
- `UpdateQuantity` removes items when quantity hits zero or below
- `ItemCount` sums all quantities, not unique items
- `Clear` resets the map entirely

I showed the bugs to the team. We all fix them together. The map stores pointers so we can modify quantities in place. The `GetItems` method returns copies so external code can't corrupt our friends inside.

No extra features. No validation beyond what tests require. Just enough genetic code to make the organism viable.

